> I have a cracked Tesla screen. It was the main screen for my Tesla, but I got a replacement for it on eBay. The glass on the screen is cracked and there is no responsiveness to touch any more, but the underlying display is perfect. Is there a market for this kind of cracked screen?
**assistant**:
> Yes—there’s a modest market for it as a **“for parts or repair” Tesla MCU/display assembly**, especially if the LCD image, backlight, and housing/connectors work.
> 
> Potential buyers include:
> - Repair shops that replace the touch digitizer/glass.
> - DIY Tesla owners.
> - Electronics recyclers or parts resellers.
> - People needing the display panel or internal boards rather than touch function.
> 
> List it on eBay, Tesla-owner forums, or Facebook Marketplace with the exact vehicle/year and screen part number. Use a title like:
> 
> > Tesla Model [S/3/X/Y] Center Touchscreen Display — LCD Works, Glass Cracked / Touch Dead — Parts Repair
> 
> Include photos/video showing the screen powered on, plus all labels and connectors. Be explicit that it is **not touch-responsive** and may require programming/pairing depending on the model.
> 
> Its value will be far below a working unit, but it is usually worth listing rather than trashing—often roughly **$50–250**, with newer/larger screens and rarer models potentially higher. Check eBay’s **Sold** listings for your exact part number before pricing.
